<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inigo Jones</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1573">1573</a>–<a href="/years/1652">1652</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Inigo Jones was an English architect, the first to introduce the classical architecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the first there to employ Vitruvian rules of proportion and symmetry. Born in Smithfield, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, he first became known as a designer of costumes and scenery for masques, many with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ben-jonson">Ben Jonson</a>, and is credited with bringing movable scenery and the proscenium arch to English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. Appointed Surveyor of the King's Works in 1613, he designed the Queen's House at Greenwich, the first English building in a pure classical style, the Banqueting House in Whitehall and the layout of Covent Garden. His career effectively ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> in 1642.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inigo-jones.jpg" alt="Inigo Jones" width="330" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Anthony van Dyck / William Hogarth · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PortraitInigoJones.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Inigo Jones</b> thought it Roman, and John Aubrey and William Stukeley linked it to the Druids.</blockquote><a href="/work/stonehenge">Stonehenge · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
